<fragment>
    <!--
        Fragment: Enforce Agent Limits
        Purpose: Depth and fan-out guards for agent/sub-agent/MCP-tool
                 call chains (guides/agent-hierarchy-attribution.md,
                 section 5). MUST run after frag-resolve-agent-hierarchy
                 (needs agentRootId/agentDepth/agentTrustTier) — include
                 this fragment immediately after that one.

        No-op entirely for ordinary, non-hierarchical requests
        (agentTrustTier == "None", the vast majority of traffic) — both
        checks below are skipped, not evaluated-and-passed, so this adds
        zero overhead to the common case.

        DEPTH — genuinely log-only until you flip the switch:
        {{agent-limits-enforce}} named value (default "false") gates
        whether an over-depth request is actually rejected or just
        traced as a warning. Per the design doc's own "observe before
        enforce" posture: run in log-only mode first, look at the real
        depth distribution your actual agent traffic produces via the
        Agent-Depth-Exceeded trace source, THEN pick a real
        {{agent-max-depth}} threshold and flip the switch — a guessed
        threshold enforced from day one either blocks legitimate deep
        workflows or is set so loose it catches nothing.

        Rejection is a structured, distinctly-coded 400
        (agent_depth_exceeded) — deliberately NOT 429/5xx, which a naive
        retry loop would treat as transient and hammer again. A
        depth-limit rejection is non-transient: retrying the identical
        call hits the identical rejection.

        FAN-OUT — a real technical constraint, stated plainly rather than
        faked: APIM's quota-by-key has no "count but don't block" mode —
        it always rejects once the counter is exceeded, so there is no
        way to make this genuinely log-only the way the depth check
        above is. The honest rollout strategy instead: deploy with a
        deliberately generous {{agent-max-fanout}} initially, watch the
        quota-exceeded alert rate (throttling-events-handling.md already
        covers this alert category), and tighten the threshold once you
        have real traffic data — same "observe before enforce" goal,
        different mechanism because the underlying primitive doesn't
        support the same rollout pattern the depth check does.

        Counter TTL ({{agent-fanout-window-seconds}}, default 3600 =
        1 hour) doubles as the orphaned-counter safety net the design doc
        calls for (section 5's "a crashed sub-agent won't decrement its
        own count") — quota-by-key's own renewal-period IS the reset,
        no separate cleanup job needed.

        Named values required (create these against your deployment):
        - {{agent-max-depth}}: integer, e.g. "10"
        - {{agent-max-fanout}}: integer, e.g. "50"
        - {{agent-fanout-window-seconds}}: integer, e.g. "3600"
        - {{agent-limits-enforce}}: "true" | "false" (default posture:
          create it set to "false" until you've observed real traffic)

        HONESTY NOTE: not exercised against a live APIM instance in this
        session.
    -->

    <choose>
        <when condition="@((string)context.Variables["agentTrustTier"] != "None")">

            <!-- Depth check — log-only or enforcing, per {{agent-limits-enforce}}. -->
            <choose>
                <when condition="@(int.Parse((string)context.Variables["agentDepth"]) > {{agent-max-depth}})">
                    <choose>
                        <when condition="@(bool.Parse("{{agent-limits-enforce}}"))">
                            <return-response>
                                <set-status code="400" reason="Bad Request" />
                                <set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
                                    <value>application/json</value>
                                </set-header>
                                <set-body>@{
                                    return new JObject(
                                        new JProperty("error", "agent_depth_exceeded"),
                                        new JProperty("message", $"Agent call chain depth {context.Variables["agentDepth"]} exceeds the configured maximum ({{agent-max-depth}}). This is not a transient error — retrying the identical call will fail again."),
                                        new JProperty("agentRootId", (string)context.Variables["agentRootId"]),
                                        new JProperty("agentDepth", (string)context.Variables["agentDepth"])
                                    ).ToString();
                                }</set-body>
                            </return-response>
                        </when>
                        <otherwise>
                            <trace source="Agent-Depth-Exceeded" severity="warning">
                                <message>@($"LOG-ONLY (agent-limits-enforce=false): would have rejected — agentRootId={context.Variables["agentRootId"]} agentDepth={context.Variables["agentDepth"]} max={{agent-max-depth}}")</message>
                            </trace>
                        </otherwise>
                    </choose>
                </when>
            </choose>

            <!-- Fan-out check — always enforcing (see HONESTY NOTE above
                 on why this one can't be log-only). Only reached if the
                 depth check above didn't already return a response. -->
            <quota-by-key
                counter-key="@((string)context.Variables["agentRootId"] + ":fanout")"
                calls="{{agent-max-fanout}}"
                renewal-period="{{agent-fanout-window-seconds}}"
                increment-condition="@(true)" />
        </when>
    </choose>
</fragment>
